      "text": "To determine the sum of the positive divisors of \\(400\\), we first need to find its prime factorization. The number \\(400\\) can be factored as follows:\n\n\\[ 400 = 8 \\times 50 = 8 \\times (25 \\times 2) = 8 \\times (5^2 \\times 2) = (2^3 \\times 5^2) \\times 2 = 2^4 \\times 5^2 \\]\n\nThe number of divisors of a number \\(p^a \\times q^b\\) is given by \\((a+1)(b+1)\\). For \\(400 = 2^4 \\times 5^2\\), we have:\n\n\\[\n(4+1)(2+1) = 5 \\times 3 = 15 \\text{ divisors}\n\\]\n\nThe divisors of \\(400\\) are \\(1, 2, 4, 5, 8, 10, 16, 20, 25, 40, 50, 80, 100, 200, 400\\).\n\nTo find the sum of the divisors of \\(400\\), we use the formula for the sum of divisors of the form \\(p^a \\times q^b\\):\n\n\\[\n\\sigma(p^a \\times q^b) = (1 + p + p^2 + \\cdots + p^a)(1 + q + q^2 + \\cdots + q^b)\n\\]\n\nFor \\(400 = 2^4 \\times 5^2\\):\n\n\\[\n\\sigma(400) = \\sigma(2^4) \\times \\sigma(5^2)\n\\]\n\nThe sum of the divisors of \\(2^4\\) is:\n\n\\[\n1 + 2 + 2^2 + 2^3 + 2^4 = 1 + 2 + 4 + 8 + 16 = 31\n\\]\n\nThe sum of the divisors of \\(5^2\\) is:\n\n\\[\n1 + 5 + 5^2 = 1 + 5 + 25 = 31\n\\]\n\nThus, the sum of the divisors of \\(400\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\sigma(400) = 31 \\times 31 = 961\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to determine how many distinct prime factors \\(961\\) has. Since \\(961 = 31^2\\), the prime factor of \\(961\\) is \\(31\\). Therefore, the number of distinct prime factors in the sum of the positive divisors of \\(400\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{1}\n\\]",
